/** Standalone batch AO -- new 4-case standard (Sample + boundary + tricky + stress).
 * All solvers below were independently written and verified byte-for-byte against each problem's
 * real official Sample before being trusted to generate boundary/tricky/stress expected output,
 * EXCEPT uva-12546 whose official Sample has one spurious blank line (see below).
 *
 * NOTE: uva-13211-geonosis was investigated extensively (14+ candidate cost-accounting formulas for
 * the "power needed to destroy towers in a given order" rule, including global vs. subgraph-
 * constrained shortest paths, single- vs. bidirectional summation, symmetrized vs. as-given directed
 * weights, and multiple variations of which alive-set a destruction step's cost should be computed
 * over) without finding an exact match against the real sample's first dataset -- deferred alongside
 * uva-798/uva-1101/uva-1203 rather than risk seeding incorrect data.
 *
 * uva-13242-pool-filling: O(n^2) prefix-sum scan over all contiguous jar ranges (n<=3000, trivial at
 *   that scale), tracking the range minimizing |avgTemp-target| subject to volume in [capacity/2,
 *   capacity] and |avgTemp-target|<=5, tie-broken toward the lexicographically smallest (i,j) --
 *   verified directly against the real sample's own first dataset (jars 1,2,3 tie with the full range
 *   0..2, both giving the exact target temperature, and the shorter/earlier range 0-1 wins).
 * uva-12602-nice-licence-plates: base-26 letter-value decode (A=0..Z=25) vs. the 4-digit number,
 *   nice iff |diff|<=100. Verified against all 50 datasets in the real sample.
 * uva-12654-patches: circular interval-covering DP -- since an optimal solution's patches can always
 *   be assumed to start exactly at a hole position (sliding a patch's start forward to the next hole
 *   never increases cost or loses coverage), try every hole as the "cut point" breaking the circle
 *   into a line (via a doubled position array), then run a linear DP that always places the next
 *   patch (length T1 or T2) starting at the next uncovered hole. A bug caught here: patch coverage
 *   must be INCLUSIVE (a length-k patch covers two holes exactly k apart, per the problem's own
 *   explicit rule) -- using a half-open interval undercounted coverage and produced systematically
 *   worse (larger) totals. Verified against all 12 datasets in the real sample.
 * uva-12797-letters: since a "consistent path" fixes, for each of the 10 possible letters, a single
 *   global choice of lowercase-only or uppercase-only (or the letter simply doesn't appear on the
 *   path), try all 2^10=1024 such assignments, BFS the resulting walkable subgrid for each, and take
 *   the minimum shortest path over all assignments that connect the two corners. A bug caught here:
 *   the problem's "length" counts CELLS visited, not edges traversed -- verified against the problem
 *   statement's own worked 6x6 example (length 13) and all 18 datasets in the real sample.
 * uva-12694-meeting-room-arrangement: classic activity-selection greedy (sort by finish time, take
 *   an event iff its start is >= the last taken event's finish). Verified against all 18 datasets in
 *   the real sample.
 * uva-12970-alcoholic-pilots: direct BigInt fraction arithmetic (captain's plane time d1/v1 vs. the
 *   competitor's d2/v2, compared via cross-multiplication to avoid floating point; average time as an
 *   exact reduced fraction via GCD). Verified against all 25 datasets in the real sample, including
 *   several with denominators in the billions.
 * uva-13190-rockabye-tobby: classic k-way merge via a binary min-heap keyed by (next dose time,
 *   medication priority index), popping k times and re-inserting each medication's next multiple.
 *   Verified against the real sample's full multi-dataset trace.
 * uva-12882-city-park: union-find over axis-aligned non-overlapping rectangles, detecting adjacency
 *   via coordinate-grouped edge matching -- for each x value shared by some rectangles' right edges
 *   and others' left edges, the two edge-interval lists are each internally NON-OVERLAPPING (since the
 *   rectangles themselves don't overlap), enabling an O(k log k) two-pointer sweep per group instead
 *   of an O(k^2) pairwise check; symmetric handling for horizontal (top/bottom) adjacency. Verified
 *   against the real sample's own 8-stone dataset (matching the problem's own figure explanation:
 *   largest surface area 20, from stones 7 and 1).
 * uva-12546-lcm-pair-sum: derived a closed-form multiplicative formula -- f(n) = A(n) + n, where A(n)
 *   (sum over ordered divisor pairs (p,q) with lcm(p,q)=n of p) factors as a product over each prime
 *   power p^a in n's factorization of [(p^a-1)/(p-1) + (a+1)*p^a], computed mod 1e9+7 via modular
 *   inverse. THE SCRAPED OFFICIAL SAMPLE HAS A SPURIOUS BLANK LINE between "Case 2" and "Case 3" --
 *   the underlying VALUES (41, 117, 16) were independently confirmed correct by hand against the
 *   problem statement's own worked example (f(6)=41) before this formatting artifact was found.
 *   sampleLimit: 0.
 */
